2186045417] [17592186199052 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1061 144910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "62147a48-4a50-55e4-a548-b950bc9af5a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199053 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1061 144911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f2be205-1493-56f5-8512-a42f382c50fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199054 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1061 144912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e2b4e61-5f88-5898-bcf9-9d4fae86c41e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199055 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198260 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1061 144915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b35ba584-56c8-537f-b6d7-15de6671592a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199056 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1061 144916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bc5d356c-d8d2-517d-839c-18b823820728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199057 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198261 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Note that we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **implication** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Rightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis implication is false whenever $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true *and* $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is false for a specific set of respective variable assignments $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n.$\n\nNow, recall the following logical equivalence:\n\n$$\nP\\Rightarrow Q \\equiv \\lnot P \\lor Q\n$$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of $P \\Rightarrow Q$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose we have the following universal set:\n\n$$\nU = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nLet's define the following predicates over $U{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is prime\n\n- $Q(x):$ $9$ is divisible by $x$\n\nTo find the truth set of $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x),$ we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = \\left\\{ 2,3,5,7 \\right\\}.$ As a result, the complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=\\overset{―}{\\{2,3,5,7\\}}=\\{1,4,6,8,9\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,3,9 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =\\{1,4,6,8,9\\}\\cup \\{1,3,9\\} \\\\  & =\\{1,3,4,6,8,9\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1065 7992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "06a295ff-82fc-5afe-8aa1-b24421f2c474" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199525 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1064 "Since implication and equivalence of predicates are simply generalizations of logical operations on statements, all the properties of these operations can be translated to the corresponding operations on predicates.\n\nIn particular, for predicates $P(x_1,x_2\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(y_1,y_2\\ldots,y_m),$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P⇒Q & ≡¬P∨Q \\\\ P⇔Q & ≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∨Q)∧(¬Q∨P) \\\\  & ≡(¬P∧¬Q)∨(P∧Q)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's remind ourselves of the grammatical constructions for implications and biconditionals that we've encountered so far:\n\nFor conditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Rightarrow Q$\n\n- *If $P,$ then $Q$*\n\n- *$P$ implies $Q$*\n\nFor biconditional statements, the following are equivalent:\n\n- $P\\Leftrightarrow Q$\n\n- *$P$ is equivalent to $Q$*\n\n- *$P$ if and only if $Q$*\n\n- *If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1065 7993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "599dc11e-cbd2-596c-bb72-4f73b83034ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199526 #edb/ref 1067 "Logical Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences With Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $T_P$ and $T_Q$ be the truth sets of the predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ respectively. Again, we're assuming our predicates have the same universal set.\n\nThe **equivalence** of the predicates $P$ and $Q$ is a new predicate that we denote as follows:\n\n$$\nP(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\n$$\n\nThis predicate is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth values for a specific set of variable assignments $x_1,x_2,\\ldots, x_n.$\n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)\n$$\n\nLet's look at some examples. Assume $x\\in\\mathbb R$ throughout.\n\n- The following statement is an equivalence (i.e., it is true).\n\n$$\nx + 2 = 5 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=3\n$$\n\nThis statement is true because \n\n$$\n\\left(x+2=5\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x=3\\right)\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\left(x=3\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad x+2 = 5\\right).\n$$\n\n- The following statement is **not** an equivalence (i.e., it is false).\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=2\n$$\n\nThis statement is false because \n\n$$\n\\left(x=2 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad  x^2 = 4\\right) \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\left(x^2=4\\quad \\not\\Rightarrow\\quad x=2\\right).\n$$\n\nHowever, the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nx^2 = 4 \\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad x=\\pm 2\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1065 7995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e9f21f0-8dbb-5442-968e-b1fb4425fef7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199527 #edb/ref 1067 "Equivalence of Predicates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1064 "For predicates $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ and $Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n),$ the equivalence $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)\\Leftrightarrow Q(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is true when $P$ and $Q$ have the same truth value.  \n\n$$\nP \\Leftrightarrow Q \\equiv \\big(P \\land Q\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P \\land \\neg Q\\big)\n$$\n\nIt follows that the truth set of $P \\Leftrightarrow Q$ can be found using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the truth set of $P(x) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $\\mathbb{R}{:}$\n\n- $P(x):$ $1-5x  < 11$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x \\geq -1$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the corresponding inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5x & <11 \\\\ -5x & <10 \\\\ x & >-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is $T_P = (-2, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_P = (-\\infty,-2].$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is $T_Q = [-1, \\infty)$ and its complement is $\\overline{T}_Q = (-\\infty,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =(-2,\\infty )\\cap [-1,\\infty ) \\\\  & =[-1,\\infty ) \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cap (-\\infty ,-1) \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-2] \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\  & =[-1,\\infty )\\cup (-\\infty ,-2] \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [-1,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1065 9146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "196ed409-81bb-5e86-bf1c-5e34aa4fb041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199603 #edb/ref 1067 "Truth Sets of Equivalences" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381665 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381665 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381665 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381665 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\,$ If $x$ has more than three divisors, then $x$ is composite.\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381665 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7487ada-70fd-5ba0-8873-b0b458efd67c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381665 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ has more than three divisors.\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is composite.\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x$ doesn't have more than three divisors.\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, or $x$ is composite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381668 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186381671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381666 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bdd2d766-2bf1-5ae1-be67-0595eea264b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381667 #edb/ref 1080 "$x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, and $x$ is composite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1bebf7f-222e-5891-9936-432740b170c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381668 #edb/ref 1080 "$x$ has more than three divisors, or $x$ is composite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381668 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381668 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "73ad96be-7b66-5c32-81d0-4e3629567422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381669 #edb/ref 1080 "$x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, or $x$ is not composite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381669 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381669 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6eec7d7d-14dd-518a-bd3a-7698e21feab4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381670 #edb/ref 1080 "$x$ has more than three divisors, and $x$ is composite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381670 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381670 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3a49ba8-c855-5597-9bac-312a8943d425" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381671 #edb/ref 1080 "$x$ doesn't have more than three divisors, or $x$ is composite." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381671 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381671 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3b717337-2c58-5979-bdc9-3a799fc95640" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381679 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381679 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183357" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381679 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381679 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $3\\,|\\,x.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $5\\,|x.$\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ or if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381679 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9aa776da-0143-5b4b-9d08-2751ea2726a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381679 #edb/ref 1074 "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ if and only if $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- $3\\,|\\,x$ is equivalent to $3\\,|\\,(x+15).$\n\n- If $3\\,|\\,x$ then $3\\,|\\,(x+15),$ and if $3\\,|\\,(x+15)$ then $3\\,|\\,x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186381685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186381683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381680 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c8661f64-c8fd-5a5e-8d68-13ad71e9e23d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381681 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381681 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381681 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24f5e0da-6ff9-54f8-9899-0fcb4faf174b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381682 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381682 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381682 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6ba148f2-8aed-5afe-b8f6-2d6df1077df1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381683 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381683 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381683 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2514634f-1c9a-516d-9f01-6fcf8e8bb2ef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381684 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381684 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381684 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eb6bb8e3-7838-5de4-b233-6ca16778703e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381685 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381685 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381685 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d12b87f7-5b3d-5c51-b825-9978e6d5f710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381686 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186381687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381686 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183383" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381686 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381686 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/17/17a493e38294911702a94b8755111db85e7445f03d4d149602b57eebf009f14b.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of predicates $P(x)$ and $Q(x),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P(x)⇔Q(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381686 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96ab9b12-c6bc-56bd-aeba-ea72894263c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186381686 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we have that \n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set ${T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q}:$\n\n![](images/e9/e995ce9652d431c39c7a629a5e825c91fcdb2da56b83ad371c6bb319bc548eb9.png)\n\nThen, we sketch t